.TH "libpath" "7" "July 2026" "BetterScripts libpath v1.0.0" "Path helpers for POSIX.1 shell scripts."
.hy
.SS \f[V]libpath.sh\f[R]
.SS SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[I]Full synopsis, description, arguments, examples and other
information is\f[R] \f[I]documented with each individual command.\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]path_basename <VARIABLE> [--] <PATH> [<SUFFIX>]\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]path_dirname <VARIABLE> [--] <PATH>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]path_pwd <VARIABLE> [<OPTIONS>...]\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]path_serial [-L|--dereference|--follow-links] [--] [<VARIABLE>] <PATH>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]path_size [-L|--dereference|--follow-links] [--] [<VARIABLE>] <PATH>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]path_owner [-L|--dereference|--follow-links] [-n|--name] [--] [<VARIABLE>] <PATH>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]path_mode [-L|--dereference|--follow-links] [-c|--chmod] [--] [<VARIABLE>] <PATH>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]path_mktemp [<OPTIONS>...] [--] [<TEMPLATE>]\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]path_mktmp [<OPTIONS>...] [--] [<TEMPLATE>]\f[R]
.SS DESCRIPTION
.PP
Provides commands to allow any \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] compliant shell to
process paths safely and robustly.
.SS EXIT STATUS
.IP \[bu] 2
For all commands the exit status will be \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R])
if, and only if, the command was completed successfully.
.IP \[bu] 2
For any command which is intended to perform a test, an exit status of
\f[V]1\f[R] (\f[V]<one>\f[R]) indicates \[lq]false\[rq], while
\f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]) indicates \[lq]true\[rq].
.IP \[bu] 2
An exit status that is \f[I]NOT\f[R] \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]) from
an external command will be propagated to the caller where relevant (and
possible).
.IP \[bu] 2
Exit status\[cq] not covered by any of the above use values as described
in FreeBSD
\f[V]SYSEXITS(3)\f[R] (https://www.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?sysexits(3))
- including the \f[V]EX_USAGE\f[R] which is used for all usage errors.
.IP \[bu] 2
Exit status is configuration agnostic.
.SS ENVIRONMENT
.PP
A number of environment variables affect the library, these are split
into variables that instruct the library to work-around specific
platform issues, and variables that convey user preferences.
Variables that enable platform specific work-arounds will be
automatically set if needed, but can also be set manually to force
specific configurations.
.PP
In additional to these, there are a number of variables that are set by
the library to convey information outside of command invocation.
.PP
If unset, some variables will take an initial value from a common
\f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] variable applicable to all libraries, these allow
the same configuration to be used across libraries more easily.
.PP
After the library has been sourced, external commands must not set
library environment variables that are classified as \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R].
Variables may use the \f[V]readonly\f[R] command to enforce this.
.PP
\f[B]\f[BI]If not otherwise specified, an \f[VBI]<unset>\f[BI] variable
is equivalent to the\f[B]\f[R] \f[B]\f[BI]default value.\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[I]For more details see the \f[VI]shtoolkit\f[I] general
documentation.\f[R]
.SS PLATFORM CONFIGURATION
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_NO_Z_SHELL_SETOPT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_Z_SHELL_SETOPT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
[Disable]/Enable using \f[V]setopt\f[R] in \f[I]Z Shell\f[R] to ensure
\f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] like behavior.
.IP \[bu] 2
Automatically enabled if \f[I]Z Shell\f[R] is detected.
.IP \[bu] 2
Any use of \f[V]setopt\f[R] is scoped as tightly as possible and SHOULD
not affect other commands.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_EXTENDED_FILE_MODE_FORMAT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]TEXT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[V]posix\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Used by \f[V]path_mode\f[R] to determine how to convert modes into
\f[V]chmod\f[R] format.
.IP \[bu] 2
Some implementations extend the standard with additional file modes,
this determines if those extensions are handled or if they result in an
error.
.IP \[bu] 2
MUST be either \f[V]posix\f[R] (default) or \f[V]solaris\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]\f[VI]posix\f[I]\f[R]: use only the standard defined file modes,
anything else will cause an error for certain operations.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]solaris\f[R]: allow the \[lq]mandatory file locking\[rq] mode
extension.
.IP \[bu] 2
The default is to use \f[V]solaris\f[R] only when \f[V]uname -s\f[R] is
\f[V]SunOS\f[R], and \f[V]posix\f[R] in all other cases.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_NO_LS_Q\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
Indicates if \f[V]-q\f[R] is an accepted option for \f[V]ls\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Although \f[V]-q\f[R] is required by the standard, some implementations
do not support it (e.g.\ \f[V]busybox\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
While \f[V]-q\f[R] is never absolutely necessary, it is helpful to use
to ensure output does not cause unexpected problems.
(Generally \f[V]ls\f[R] output is parsed only for information
\f[B]not\f[R] related to the filename output.)
.SS INFORMATIONAL
.PP
Variables that convey library information.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Integer >= 1.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented when there are significant changes, or any changes break
compatibility with previous versions.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_MINOR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Integer >= 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented for significant changes that do not break compatibility with
previous versions.
.IP \[bu] 2
Reset to 0 when \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R] changes.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_PATCH\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Integer >= 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented for minor revisions or bugfixes.
.IP \[bu] 2
Reset to 0 when \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_MINOR\f[R] changes.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_RELEASE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
A string indicating a pre-release version, always null for full-release
versions.
.IP \[bu] 2
Possible values include `alpha', `beta', `rc', etc, (a numerical suffix
may also be appended).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_FULL\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Full version combining \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R],
\f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_MINOR\f[R], and
\f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_PATCH\f[R] as a single integer.
.IP \[bu] 2
Can be used in numerical comparisons
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[V]MNNNPPP\f[R] where, \f[V]M\f[R] is the \f[V]MAJOR\f[R]
version, \f[V]NNN\f[R] is the \f[V]MINOR\f[R] version (3 digit, zero
padded), and \f[V]PPP\f[R] is the \f[V]PATCH\f[R] version (3 digit, zero
padded).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Full version combining \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R],
\f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_MINOR\f[R], \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_PATCH\f[R],
and \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_VERSION_RELEASE\f[R] as a formatted string.
.IP \[bu] 2
Format:
\f[V]BetterScripts \[aq]libpath\[aq] vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH[-RELEASE]\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Derived tools MUST include unique identifying information in this value
that differentiates them from the BetterScripts versions.
(This information should precede the version number.)
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_LAST_ERROR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Stores the error message of the most recent error.
.IP \[bu] 2
ONLY valid immediately following a command for which the exit status is
not \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
Available even when error output is suppressed.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_SOURCED\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Set (and non-null) once the library has been sourced.
.IP \[bu] 2
Dependant scripts can query if this variable is set to determine if this
file has been sourced.
.SS USER PREFERENCE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_QUIET_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_QUIET_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
[Enable]/Disable library error message output.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: error messages will be written to \f[V]STDERR\f[R] as:
\f[V][libpath::<COMMAND>]: ERROR: <MESSAGE>\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: library error messages will be suppressed.
.IP \[bu] 2
The most recent error message is always available in
\f[V]BS_LIBPATH_LAST_ERROR\f[R] even when error output is suppressed.
.IP \[bu] 2
Both the library version of this option and the suite version can be
modified between command invocations and will affect the next command.
.IP \[bu] 2
Does NOT affect errors from non-library commands, which \f[I]may\f[R]
still produce output.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_FATAL_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_FATAL_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Enable/[Disable] causing library errors to terminate the current
(sub-)shell.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: errors stop any further processing, and cause a non-zero
exit status, but do not cause an exception.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: any library error will cause an \[lq]unset variable\[rq]
shell exception using the
\f[V]${parameter:?[word]}\f[R] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_06_02)
parameter expansion, where \f[V]word\f[R] is set to an error message
that \f[I]should\f[R] be displayed by the shell (this message is NOT
suppressed by \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_QUIET_ERRORS\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
Both the library version of this option and the suite version can be
modified between command invocations and will affect the next command.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_PREFER_HEXDUMP\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_PREFER_HEXDUMP\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Enable/[Disable] using \f[V]hexdump\f[R] in preference to \f[V]od\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use \f[V]od\f[R] whenever possible.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use \f[V]hexdump\f[R] even if \f[V]od\f[R] is available.
.IP \[bu] 2
Only affects \f[V]path_mktemp\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Has no effect unless both \f[V]od\f[R] and \f[V]hexdump\f[R] are
available.
.IP \[bu] 2
Some systems lack \f[V]od\f[R], but provide \f[V]hexdump\f[R] for the
same purpose, while many provide both.
This flag allows the use of either \f[V]od\f[R] or \f[V]hexdump\f[R] to
be preferred.
.IP \[bu] 2
The default is to use \f[V]od\f[R] if available and \f[V]hexdump\f[R]
otherwise.
If neither command is available different (situation dependent) methods
for generating data are used.
.IP \[bu] 2
See also: \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_RANDOM_SOURCE\f[R], and
\f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_NO_MKTEMP\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_RANDOM_SOURCE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_RANDOM_SOURCE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]TEXT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[V]/dev/urandom\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Specify a source for random data.
.IP \[bu] 2
Only affects \f[V]path_mktemp\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
MUST be either the special value \f[V]awk\f[R] or a path to use as a
source for random data.
.IP \[bu] 2
If specified as a path, the path MUST be readable, and MUST behave like
\f[V]/dev/urandom\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
If specified as \f[V]awk\f[R], random data is generated by \f[V]awk\f[R]
- \f[B]this is insecure\f[R] as the data generated is of poor quality
and likely to be easy to guess.
.IP \[bu] 2
Has no effect if neither \f[V]od\f[R] nor \f[V]hexdump\f[R] is
available.
.IP \[bu] 2
WARNING: \f[V]/dev/urandom\f[R] \f[B]can\f[R] block in some
circumstances - when this may occur varies by system.
.IP \[bu] 2
See also: \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_PREFER_HEXDUMP\f[R], and
\f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_NO_MKTEMP\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_NO_MKTEMP\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
[Enable]/Disable allowing the use of \f[V]mktemp\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: allow \f[V]mktemp\f[R] to be used when possible.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: force \f[V]mktemp\f[R] to never be used, even if it is
available.
.IP \[bu] 2
Only affects \f[V]path_mktemp\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Has no effect if \f[V]mktemp\f[R] is not available.
.IP \[bu] 2
Functionality of \f[V]mktemp\f[R] implementations varies, and even when
available it may not be possible to use \f[V]mktemp\f[R] in all cases.
For example, GNU
\f[V]mktemp\f[R] (https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/mktemp-invocation.html)
provides more functionality than implementations such as
BSD (https://man.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?mktemp(1))
variants (https://man.openbsd.org/mktemp.1).
.IP \[bu] 2
See also: \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_PREFER_HEXDUMP\f[R], and
\f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_RANDOM_SOURCE\f[R].
.SS COMMANDS
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]path_basename\f[R]
.PP
Set a variable to the value from \f[V]basename\f[R] for a specified
path, without potentially losing data if the path ends
\f[V]<newline>\f[R] characters.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
path_basename <VARIABLE> [--] <PATH> [<SUFFIX>]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]VARIABLE\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the output.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
.TP
\f[V]PATH\f[R] [in]
Path to process.
.TP
\f[V]SUFFIX\f[R] [in]
Suffix to remove.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
path_basename File -- \[dq]$0\[dq] \[dq].sh\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
All arguments except the first are passed directly to \f[V]basename\f[R]
- supported arguments and values are determined by the implementation of
that utility; any non-standard options may be used.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]path_dirname\f[R]
.PP
Set a variable to the value from \f[V]dirname\f[R] for a specified path,
without potentially losing data if the path ends \f[V]<newline>\f[R]
characters.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
path_dirname <VARIABLE> [--] <PATH>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]VARIABLE\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the output.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
.TP
\f[V]PATH\f[R] [in]
Path to process.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
path_dirname Dir -- \[dq]$0\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
All arguments except the first are passed directly to \f[V]dirname\f[R]
- supported arguments and values are determined by the implementation of
that utility; any non-standard options may be used.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]path_pwd\f[R]
.PP
Set a variable to the value from \f[V]pwd\f[R], without potentially
losing data if the path ends \f[V]<newline>\f[R] characters.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
path_pwd <VARIABLE> [<OPTIONS>...]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]VARIABLE\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the output.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
path_pwd CurrentDir
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
The output from \f[V]pwd\f[R] is defined in the standard in terms of the
environment variable \f[V]PWD\f[R] (i.e.\ \f[V]pwd\f[R] is supposed to
return the value stored in \f[V]PWD\f[R]).
However, the value contained in \f[V]PWD\f[R] can be modified directly
by scripts and so \f[I]may\f[R] not actually contain the current path.
(What occurs when assigning to \f[V]PWD\f[R] is implementation defined.)
If \f[V]PWD\f[R] is not the current directory, the output of
\f[V]pwd\f[R] is \f[I]not\f[R] specified by the standard, however, most
implementations seem to do something sensible.
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Any arguments after the first are passed directly to \f[V]pwd\f[R] -
supported arguments and values are determined by the implementation of
that utility; any non-standard options may be used.
.IP \[bu] 2
Standard \f[V]pwd\f[R] supports \f[V]-L\f[R] and \f[V]-P\f[R] options,
with no option being equivalent to \f[V]-L\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]path_serial\f[R]
.PP
Get the serial for the given path.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
path_serial [-L|--dereference|--follow-links] [--] [<VARIABLE>] <PATH>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]-L\f[R], \f[V]--dereference\f[R], \f[V]--follow-links\f[R] [in]
If \f[V]PATH\f[R] is a symbolic link, get the information for the link
target.
If not specified, the information will be for the symbolic link itself.
If the symbolic link is broken or inaccessible, using this option will
result in an error.
Must be specified before any other arguments.
.TP
\f[V]VARIABLE\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the serial.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name or a \f[V]-\f[R]
(\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]).
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R])
serial is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]PATH\f[R] [in]
\f[V]PATH\f[R] to get a serial for.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Serial=$(path_serial \[dq]$File\[dq])
path_serial \[aq]Serial\[aq] \[dq]$File\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
A \[lq]File Serial
Number\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap03.html#tag_03_175)
is defined as: \f[I]\[lq]A per-file system unique identifier for a
file.\[rq]\f[R] and can \f[B]not\f[R] uniquely identify a file for an
entire system.
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
The serial will be an unsigned integer, but has an \[lq]implementation
defined\[rq] size.
.IP \[bu] 2
The value reported here is the only value guaranteed to be available in
a \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] shell.
The commonly available \f[V]stat\f[R] command is able to provide much
more useful (and accurate) information, but is not standard, and varies
in usage between implementations.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]path_size\f[R]
.PP
Get the size of given file.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
path_size [-L|--dereference|--follow-links] [--] [<VARIABLE>] <PATH>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]-L\f[R], \f[V]--dereference\f[R], \f[V]--follow-links\f[R] [in]
If \f[V]PATH\f[R] is a symbolic link, get the information for the link
target.
If not specified, the information will be for the symbolic link itself.
If the symbolic link is broken or inaccessible, using this option will
result in an error.
Must be specified before any other arguments.
.TP
\f[V]VARIABLE\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the size.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name or a \f[V]-\f[R]
(\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]).
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]) size
is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]PATH\f[R] [in]
\f[V]PATH\f[R] to get a size for.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Size=$(path_size -L \[dq]$File\[dq])
path_size \[aq]Size\[aq] \[dq]$File\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
If \f[V]PATH\f[R] is not an ordinary file (e.g.\ a character special or
block special file), the results are \[lq]implementation defined\[rq]
and so non-portable.
.IP \[bu] 2
Size is reported as \f[B]the number of 1024 byte blocks consumed by the
file\f[R] i.e.\ the file size will be rounded up to the nearest 1024
bytes.
.IP \[bu] 2
For some file systems or implementations, the number of blocks consumed
may not match the true file size (e.g.\ a file system that utilizes
compression may report a value significantly smaller than the size of
the uncompressed data).
.IP \[bu] 2
Files of size zero bytes \f[I]may\f[R] give non-zero output depending on
both the implementation of \f[V]ls\f[R] \f[I]and\f[R] the underlying
file system.
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
The value reported here is the only value guaranteed to be available in
a \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] shell.
The commonly available \f[V]stat\f[R] command is able to provide much
more useful (and accurate) information, but is not standard, and varies
in usage between implementations.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]path_owner\f[R]
.PP
Get the owner of a given path in the form \f[V]<OWNER>:<GROUP>\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
path_owner [-L|--dereference|--follow-links] [-n|--name] [--] [<VARIABLE>] <PATH>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]-L\f[R], \f[V]--dereference\f[R], \f[V]--follow-links\f[R] [in]
If \f[V]PATH\f[R] is a symbolic link, get the information for the link
target.
If not specified, the information will be for the symbolic link itself.
If the symbolic link is broken or inaccessible, using this option will
result in an error.
Must be specified before non-option arguments.
.TP
\f[V]-n\f[R], \f[V]--name\f[R] [in]
Report the owner and group in name format rather than numeric format.
Must be specified before non-option arguments.
.TP
\f[V]VARIABLE\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the owner.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name or a \f[V]-\f[R]
(\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]).
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]) owner
is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]PATH\f[R] [in]
\f[V]PATH\f[R] to get the owner for.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Owner=$(path_owner -Ln \[dq]$File\[dq])
path_owner \[aq]Owner\[aq] \[dq]$File\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
The owner is reported as \f[V]<UID>:<GID>\f[R] where both IDs are
numeric unless the name is explicitly requested, and a name for the
given numeric ID can be obtained.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]path_mode\f[R]
.PP
Get the \[lq]mode\[rq] (i.e.\ the permissions) of a path (in
\f[I]symbolic\f[R] format).
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
path_mode [-L|--dereference|--follow-links] [-c|--chmod] [--] [<VARIABLE>] <PATH>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]-L\f[R], \f[V]--dereference\f[R], \f[V]--follow-links\f[R] [in]
If \f[V]PATH\f[R] is a symbolic link, get the information for the link
target.
If not specified, the information will be for the symbolic link itself.
If the symbolic link is broken or inaccessible, using this option will
result in an error.
Must be specified before non-option arguments.
.TP
\f[V]-c\f[R], \f[V]--chmod\f[R] [in]
Convert the mode to the format:
\f[V]u=<user mode>,g=<group mode>,o=<other mode>[,+t]\f[R] which is
suitable for use with \f[V]chmod\f[R].
The resulting mode will be of variable length, without this option the
mode is always exactly \f[B]9\f[R] characters.
May fail with an error if non-standard mode characters are set for
\f[V]PATH\f[R].
Must be specified before non-option arguments.
.TP
\f[V]VARIABLE\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the mode.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name or a \f[V]-\f[R]
(\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]).
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]) mode
is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]PATH\f[R] [in]
A path to get the mode for.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
chmod \[dq]$(path_mode -Lc \[dq]$Reference\[dq])\[dq] \[dq]$Target\[dq]
path_mode \[aq]Mode\[aq] \[dq]$File\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
How the mode is converted to \f[V]chmod\f[R] format is dependent on
\f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_EXTENDED_FILE_MODE_FORMAT\f[R], which will
determine if non-standard mode characters result in errors.
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
The resulting mode is \f[I]always\f[R] in symbolic format as this is the
only format that can be determined with standard defined tools.
Converting this to octal format is relatively easy if required.
.IP \[bu] 2
Normally the mode output will be exactly \f[B]9\f[R] characters, while
in \f[V]chmod\f[R] format it will be of variable length with a minimum
of \f[B]8\f[R] characters, and a maximum of \f[B]22\f[R] characters when
only using standard defined modes.
.IP \[bu] 2
Although variable in length, \f[V]chmod\f[R] format is often easier to
test for specific mode values, for example, checking for the \f[V]x\f[R]
mode in the default output requires checking for either \f[V]x\f[R]
\f[I]or\f[R] \f[V]s\f[R] for \[lq]user\[rq] and \[lq]group\[rq], and
\f[V]x\f[R] \f[I]or\f[R] \f[V]t\f[R] for \[lq]other\[rq], while in
\f[V]chmod\f[R] format the test is just for \f[V]x\f[R] in any of the
three groups.
.IP \[bu] 2
The value reported here is the only value guaranteed to be available in
a \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] shell.
The commonly available \f[V]stat\f[R] command is able to provide much
more useful (and accurate) information, but is not standard, and varies
in usage between implementations.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]path_mktemp\f[R]
.PP
Create a temporary path.
.PP
A drop in replacement for the \f[V]mktemp\f[R] command found on many
systems, but available even when \f[V]mktemp\f[R] is not and with system
independent functionality.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
path_mktemp
   [--target <VARIABLE>|--output <VARIABLE>]
   [-q|--quiet] [-d|--directory] [-u|--dry-run]
   [-p <DIRECTORY>|--tmpdir[=<DIRECTORY>]] [-t]
   [--suffix <SUFFIX>]
   [--]
   [<TEMPLATE>]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]--target <VARIABLE>\f[R], \f[V]--output <VARIABLE>\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will receive the generated path.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name or a \f[V]-\f[R]
(\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]).
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]) value
is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
Current contents will be lost.
.TP
\f[V]-q\f[R], \f[V]--quiet\f[R] [in]
Suppress any output while creating paths (even on error.)
.TP
\f[V]-d\f[R], \f[V]--directory\f[R] [in]
Make a directory, not a file.
.TP
\f[V]-u\f[R], \f[V]--dry-run\f[R] [in]
Do not create the path, just generate it.
This is potentially unsafe.
.TP
\f[V]-p <DIRECTORY>\f[R], \f[V]--tmpdir[=<DIRECTORY>]\f[R] [in]
The location to create the temporary item.
If not specified \f[V]DIRECTORY\f[R] is set to \f[V]TMPDIR\f[R] if it is
set, or \f[V]/tmp\f[R] otherwise.
If specified \f[V]TEMPLATE\f[R] can \f[I]not\f[R] be an absolute path
(i.e.\ can not begin with \f[V]/\f[R] (\f[V]<slash>\f[R])).
.TP
\f[V]-t\f[R] [in]
\f[V]TEMPLATE\f[R] is the final portion of a path \f[I]only\f[R].
If specified it is an error if \f[V]TEMPLATE\f[R] contains \f[I]any\f[R]
directory separators.
Implies \f[V]--tmpdir\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--suffix <SUFFIX>\f[R] [in]
Append \f[V]SUFFIX\f[R] to the final temporary path.
Can not contain any \f[V]/\f[R] (\f[V]<slash>\f[R]) characters.
Note: Usage may mean \f[V]mktemp\f[R] can not be used to generate the
final path.
.TP
\f[V]TEMPLATE\f[R] [in]
Template used to generate the temporary path.
If not specified defaults to \f[V]tmp.XXXXXXXXXX\f[R] and
\f[V]--tmpdir\f[R] is implied.
Must contain a sequence of \f[V]X\f[R] characters of length 3 or more in
the \f[I]final\f[R] path segment.
Can contain directory separators.
Valid values are modified by other arguments.
Note: If the template does \f[I]not\f[R] end in \f[V]XXX\f[R] may mean
\f[V]mktemp\f[R] can not be used to generate the final path.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
path_mktemp -dup \[ti]
path_mktemp --tmpdir a.tmp.file.XXXXXXXX
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Invokes \f[V]umask 077\f[R] before any path is created.
(This is in line with many \f[V]mktemp\f[R] implementations.)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]path_mktemp\f[R] is subject to the same safety considerations as
any implementation of \f[V]mktemp\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]Safe use of \f[VI]path_mktemp\f[I] may not be possible for some use
cases.\f[R] (This is also true of \f[V]mktemp\f[R].)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]path_mktemp\f[R] is implemented using either \f[V]mktemp\f[R],
\f[V]od\f[R], \f[V]hexdump\f[R], or \f[V]awk\f[R] depending on
configuration and availability.
(For \f[V]od\f[R] and \f[V]hexdump\f[R] \f[V]/dev/urandom\f[R] must also
be present and readable.)
.IP \[bu] 2
When \f[V]/dev/urandom\f[R] is used for random data
\f[V]path_mktemp\f[R] \f[B]may block\f[R].
Whether or not this happens is implementation and situation dependent -
some implementations never block, others only early in the boot process,
others will block whenever available entropy is too low (even though the
point of \f[V]/dev/urandom\f[R] is meant to be to avoid this).
.IP \[bu] 2
When \f[V]awk\f[R] is used for random data \f[V]path_mktemp\f[R]
\f[B]this not secure\f[R].
The random data generated is of poor quality, making the final path
potentially guessable.
.IP \[bu] 2
Even if a \f[V]mktemp\f[R] command is present on a system, it may not
support the options required to implement \f[V]path_mktemp\f[R] for
specific option combinations and values.
\f[I]In such cases \f[VI]mktemp\f[I] will \f[BI]not\f[I] be used\f[R]
even if it is used in other cases.[1]
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
The configuration variables \f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_NO_MKTEMP\f[R],
\f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_PREFER_HEXDUMP\f[R], and
\f[V]BS_LIBPATH_CONFIG_RANDOM_SOURCE\f[R] help determine how
\f[V]path_mktemp\f[R] operates.
.IP \[bu] 2
Although the \f[V]mktemp\f[R] command can be found on many systems, it
is non-standard and varies in operation between implementations,
\f[V]path_mktemp\f[R] provides identical functionality regardless of the
system \f[I]even when\f[R] \f[I]\f[VI]mktemp\f[I] is used to generate
the final path\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Functionality is largely a match for GNU
\f[V]mktemp\f[R] (https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/mktemp-invocation.html),
however there are some cases where option values considered an error for
\f[V]path_mktemp\f[R] are permitted by GNU
\f[V]mktemp\f[R] (https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/mktemp-invocation.html)
- this is a deliberate choice: while accepting these values would be
feasible it would add complexity to an already complicated command and
the GNU \f[V]mktemp\f[R] behavior seems somewhat odd, of questionable
use, and does not seem consistent with other behavior.[2]
.IP \[bu] 2
To avoid an infinite loop, a hardcoded maximum number of attempts to
generate a path is used (when \f[V]mktemp\f[R] is not used).
(The maximum is \f[V]62\[ha]3\f[R], i.e.\ \f[V]238328\f[R] - which
represents the minimum number of available paths when using the minimum
of 3 template characters represented in base-62.)
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]path_mktmp\f[R]
.PP
An alias for \f[V]path_mktemp\f[R].
.SS STANDARDS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]POSIX.1-2008\f[R] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition).
.IP \[bu] 2
FreeBSD SYSEXITS(3) (https://www.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?sysexits(3)).
.IP \[bu] 2
Semantic Versioning v2.0.0 (https://semver.org/).
.IP \[bu] 2
Inclusive Naming Initiative (https://inclusivenaming.org/).
.PP
\f[I]For more details see the \f[VI]shtoolkit\f[I] general
documentation.\f[R]
.SS CAVEATS
.IP \[bu] 2
The library attempts to account for differences between implementations
(where known), however, it is not possible to do this for every case.
.PP
\f[I]For more details see the \f[VI]shtoolkit\f[I] general
documentation.\f[R]
.SS SEE ALSO
.PP
shtoolkit(7)
.SH NOTES
.SS [1]
.PP
The BSD implementation of \f[V]mktemp\f[R], for example, lacks a
\f[V]--suffix\f[R] option, it also behaves differently if
\f[V]<TEMPLATE>\f[R] does not end with \f[V]XXX\f[R] in that it uses
\f[V]<TEMPLATE>\f[R] \f[I]as is\f[R] without injecting any random
characters.
.SS [2]
.PP
Of note, GNU
\f[V]mktemp\f[R] (https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/mktemp-invocation.html)
permits a trailing \f[V]/\f[R] (\f[V]<slash>\f[R]) to appear in
\f[V]--suffix\f[R] in some conditions where \f[V]path_mktemp\f[R] does
not.
